@synopsis
**yume** **--config** *path* [ **--validate** ] [ **--connect** *ip* ]
[ **--socks-address** *ip* ] [ **--socks-port** *port* ]
[ **--outer-carrier-evidence** *path* ]
**yume** **--config** *path* **--status**
**yume** **--config** *path* **--accept-route** *id*
**yume** **--seal-kit** *directory* **--output** *file*
**yume** **--import-kit** *file* **--into** *directory*
**yume** **--version**
**yume** **--completion** **bash**
**yume** **--help**
@end

## DESCRIPTION

**yume** is the native YUME Transport Protocol 1 client. It reads a schema-1
client configuration, maintains an authenticated session to man:yumed(8),
and runs the configured SOCKS5 and forward listeners. SOCKS5 CONNECT carries TCP streams;
UDP ASSOCIATE carries datagrams through a configured packet service. A forward
turns each connection to its loopback port or UNIX socket into a stream on its
service, optionally to a fixed destination. The server enforces each service's
destination policy.

A Linux packet adapter connects a named service to a managed IP/TUN
interface and owns its addresses, routes and optional per-link DNS. The client
is not yet qualified for production. Check **docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md**
before deployment.

With a **circuits** section, every SOCKS5 CONNECT and every forward to a
destination leaves through a circuit of two or three servers of the entry's
cluster, the entry being the configured server. The client fetches the
operator-signed routes view from the entry, chooses the other servers and
builds the circuit. UDP ASSOCIATE is refused and a packet adapter is not
allowed, since circuits carry TCP only, and plain HTTP is refused unless
**circuits.plain_http** is **allow**. When no route of **circuits.hops** can
be built, the client does not shorten it on its own: connections are refused
as not allowed until the user accepts its proposal with **--accept-route**,
unless **circuits.min_hops** approves the shorter length in advance.

## OPTIONS

@include shared/native_cli.part

Each run setting replaces one configuration key for this run only and passes
the same checks as the file's value. An error names the key's JSON pointer and
says that the value came from the command line. No run setting can change the
wire image, authentication, encryption, admission, destination policy or leak
protection. **--validate** prints every key a run setting can replace, with its
value and whether it came from the configuration or a flag.

@options
@opt **--connect** *ip*
@cli spell: --connect <ip>
@cli help: Dial this IP address instead of the host

Dial this IP literal instead of resolving the configured host, as
**endpoint.connect_address** does. The TLS server name stays the configured
host, and TLS and admission still authenticate it. Through a configured SOCKS5
upstream proxy, the proxy connects to this address.
@opt **--socks-address** *ip*
@cli spell: --socks-address <ip>
@cli help: Listen for SOCKS5 on 127.0.0.1 or ::1

Set the listen address of the configuration's only SOCKS5 adapter. The
configuration's rule applies: 127.0.0.1 or ::1.
@opt **--socks-port** *port*
@cli spell: --socks-port <port>
@cli help: Listen for SOCKS5 on this port

Set the listen port of the configuration's only SOCKS5 adapter, 1 to 65535.
@end

The status option asks the running client of the same configuration for
its state.

@options
@opt **--status**
@cli spell: --status
@cli help: Print the running client's state from its control socket

Connect to the control socket that **control.socket** names, print the
running client's state and exit. The report names the connection state, how
long it has lasted or when the next attempt starts, the server and its
verified identity, the sessions and failed attempts, payload and record bytes
in each direction, the local listeners and the last failure. The exit status
is 2 when the configuration names no control socket and 1 when no client
answers on it. **--status** takes no option besides **--config**.
With circuits, it also shows each circuit's route by name, the hop count in
use against the configured one, whether plain HTTP is refused, and any
proposal of a shorter route with its id and what that route gives up.

@opt **--accept-route** *id*
@cli spell: --accept-route <id>
@cli help: Accept the running client's proposal of a shorter route

Accept the shorter route that the running client of this configuration
proposed, when no route of **circuits.hops** could be built. The *id* is the
one **--status** shows, so an answer to an older proposal accepts nothing.
The acceptance lasts until routes of the configured length work again or
the client exits. It is the one control request that changes anything, and
only a process of the client's own user can send it. Exit statuses follow
**--status**, and an id no proposal has exits with status 2.
@end

The kit options move a client kit to another device in one file, a sealed
kit. Each pair stands alone.

@options
@opt **--seal-kit** *directory*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --seal-kit <directory>
@cli help: Seal a client kit into one file, with --output

Seal the client kit in *directory* into the file that **--output** names, and
print a new code in five groups of five characters. The kit is the
directory's regular files and those one level below it, at most 32 files of
up to 256 KiB each, and its *yume.json* must be a client configuration. The
file is Argon2id and AES-256-GCM output with no plaintext header, so it reads
as random bytes. Pass the code to the user by another channel than the file.
@opt **--output** *file*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --output <file>
@cli help: Write the sealed kit here, mode 0600, never replacing a file

Write the sealed kit to *file* with mode 0600. An existing file is not
replaced.
@opt **--import-kit** *file*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --import-kit <file>
@cli help: Open a sealed kit with its code, with --into

Open the sealed kit in *file* with the code read from standard input, echo
off on a terminal, and write it to the new directory that **--into** names.
Case, dashes and spaces in the code do not matter. A wrong code and a file
that is not a sealed kit fail the same way. The directory is written under a
private temporary name beside it and renamed into place, with mode 0700 for
directories and programs and 0600 for other files. It must not exist.
@opt **--into** *directory*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --into <directory>
@cli help: Write the imported kit to this new directory

The directory **--import-kit** creates. Its parent must exist.
@end

The evidence option observes the run and changes nothing on the wire.

@options
@opt **--outer-carrier-evidence** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --outer-carrier-evidence <path>
@cli help: Write a report on the first connection's outer carrier

When the run ends, write a report on the first connection's HTTP/2 and
WebSocket carrier to *path*, for comparison with the captured browser
session. The report holds frame types, sizes, header names with pinned or
redacted values, and timing. It holds no payload, key, address or admission
value. *path* must be absolute, in a directory this user owns that is not
group or world writable and not inside a Git worktree, and must not exist.
The file is created with mode 0600 when the run starts. The exit status is
1 when the report is incomplete, for example when the session never closed
gracefully. **--validate** does not accept this option.
@end

@include shared/native_configuration.part

## CONTROL SOCKET

@include shared/native_control.part

The client's answer holds **control** (1), **program**, **version**,
**state** (**idle**, **connecting**, **connected**, **waiting** or
**closed**), **server** with **host** and **port**, **sessions**,
**failed_attempts**, **traffic** with payload and record bytes sent and
received, **socks5** and **forwards** (the local listeners) and
**last_failure** (null, or **code** and **message**). A connected client adds
**server_identity** and **connected_ms**, and a waiting one adds
**retry_ms**. **circuits** is null without a circuits section. Otherwise it
holds **hops**, **min_hops**, **accepted_hops** (0 unless the user accepted a
shorter route), **current_hops** (0 while none can be used), **plain_http**,
**serial** of the routes view in use, **stopped** (null, or why circuits
stopped), **routes**, one per circuit with **nodes**, **age_ms** and
**streams**, and **proposal**: null, or **id**, **hops**, **nodes**,
**serial**, **gives_up** and, when measured, **latency_ms**. The client also
takes the request {"control":1,"request":"accept-route","id":ID}, which
**--accept-route** sends.

## SIGNALS

**SIGINT** and **SIGTERM** request shutdown of listeners and sessions, followed
by removal of owned TUN networking. The session closes its WebSocket the way
the captured browser does and waits up to three seconds for the server's
answer before it ends the connection.
**SIGPIPE** is ignored.

@include shared/native_exit_status.part

## EXAMPLES

Validate an existing client kit, then start its configured listeners:

@code bash
yume --config /path/to/client.json --validate
yume --config /path/to/client.json
@end

The separately installed **yume-setup** and **yume-doctor** tools
provision and inspect schema-1 kits. Their **--help** output describes their
arguments. Setup and doctor are separate programs.

